Economist Education
This section applies if you enrol in, access or participate in courses, programmes or learning services provided by Economist Education, a business of The Economist Group (“Education Services”).
How we use learner personal data
In addition to the uses described elsewhere in this Privacy Policy, we process personal data relating to learners and participants in Education Services to:
administer enrolment, account creation and access to courses and learning platforms;
deliver course content, learning activities, assessments, grading, feedback, certificates and badges;
provide learner support, service desk functions and course-related communications;
send operational and engagement communications that are necessary to deliver Education Services; and
operate, secure and improve our Education Services, including detecting and preventing fraud, misuse and academic misconduct.
Use of service providers (processors)
To deliver Education Services, The Economist Group uses specialist third-party service providers that process personal data on our behalf and in accordance with our instructions. These include providers that support:
the hosting and operation of learning platforms;
course delivery, instructional design, tutoring, assessment and learner support services; and
related administrative, technical and operational functions (such as communications and support tools).
These service providers act as data processors for the purposes of applicable data protection laws and are contractually required to:
process personal data only for the purposes of providing services to The Economist Group;
implement appropriate technical and organisational security measures; and
not use learner personal data for their own independent purposes.
International data transfers
Some service providers used in connection with Education Services process personal data outside the United Kingdom and the European Economic Area, including in South Africa and other jurisdictions where our service providers or their sub-processors operate.
Where personal data is transferred internationally, The Economist Group ensures that appropriate safeguards are in place in accordance with applicable data protection laws, such as approved standard contractual clauses or other lawful transfer mechanisms, as described in this Privacy Policy.
Identity verification
Certain Education Services may require learners to complete an identity verification check (for example, to confirm eligibility, protect the integrity of assessments or prevent misuse of certificates).
Where this applies:
learners are redirected to a third-party identity verification provider to complete the verification process under that provider’s own terms and privacy notice;
the identity verification provider acts as an independent data controller in respect of the verification process; and
The Economist Group receives only a confirmation of whether the verification has been passed or not passed, and does not receive copies of identity documents or other personal data used in the verification process.
Employer-funded Education Services
Where your participation in Education Services is purchased or funded by an employer or another organisation (an
“Employer”
), we may process and share limited learner personal data with that Employer for the purposes of administering and delivering the Education Services.
This may include, where applicable:
confirmation of enrolment or access to a course or programme;
participation or attendance status;
completion status;
detailed grades;
testimonials;
time spent on the platform;
results from module self-assessment surveys; and
confirmation that a certificate or badge has been issued.

12. What if I live in China?
If you are a resident of the People’s Republic of China (PRC) and The Economist Group processes your personal information, the Personal Information Protection Law (PIPL) applies to you. Please be aware that other parts of this privacy policy may also apply to you.
For residents of the PRC, we may use your information to:
Administer your subscription to EIU and other Economist Group products and services;
communicate with you regarding the products and services The Economist Group provides to you and send you marketing and other promotional information, with necessary lawful bases;
gather research for our EIU and other Economist Group businesses; and/or
accept and provide services to business clients in our Economist Enterprise businesses.
The Economist Group does not intentionally or actively process any sensitive information of PRC residents. In the event we do this for specific processing activities, we will provide a privacy notice.
Where we process your information, you have certain rights under the PIPL, including the:
right to know;
right to decide relating to your personal information;
right to consult and copy;
right to data portability;
right to correction;
right to deletion;
right to withdraw consent; and
right to request personal information handlers explain personal-information handling rules.
To exercise these rights, please contact
[email protected]
.
We do not actively process the information of minors under the age of 14; where we do we will seek parental consent to do so as required by law.
Transfer of your personal data outside PRC
In order to fulfil and manage the transactions of PRC residents with The Economist Group and to ensure robust information security, we may transfer some of your information we collect to our affiliate companies outside China. For our Economist Enterprise services, we host data in the United States of America. Where required, we have contractual safeguards in place and will comply with applicable legal requirements in China.
Where required, we will ask for your general or separate consent to relevant data processing activities.

13. What if I live in the United States of America?
In addition to the data subject rights documented elsewhere in this Policy, this section explains the additional privacy rights of, and our obligations for, residents of California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Iowa, Maryland, Minnesota, Montana, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Jersey, Oregon, Texas, Tennessee, Utah and Virginia.,
Sales of Personal Information and Sharing for Targeted Advertising
In some circumstances, we may ‘sell’ or ‘share’ your personal data (as defined by relevant law) with trusted third parties, subject to your right to opt out.
We may sell or share the following categories of personal data for the purposes described in our Privacy Policy, subject to your settings and preferences, and your right to opt-out:
Identifiers, such as your name and email address
Commercial Information, such as records of the services you purchase from us
The categories of third parties to whom we may sell or share the personal data includes marketing and advertising partners, analytics providers, and social networks.
Sensitive Information
The following personal data elements we collect may be classified as ‘sensitive information’ under certain privacy laws: account log-in and password, or credentials allowing access to an account. We only use or disclose sensitive personal information where such use or disclosure is necessary and proportionate for performing services you have requested and for detecting security incidents, fraud and other illegal actions. We do not collect and process sensitive information for the purpose of inferring characteristics about a consumer, and we do not sell sensitive information or process or otherwise share sensitive personal information for the purpose of targeted advertising.
Universal Opt-Out Mechanism
Where available, you may choose to enable an online tool that automatically communicates your universal opt-out preferences, such as the Global Privacy Control (‘GPC’). You may need to use a specific browser or an extension that supports these tools and universal signals.
Your Privacy Rights
In accordance with applicable privacy law, and depending upon the jurisdiction in which you reside, you may have some or all of the following rights with respect to your personal data including;
the right to obtain:
confirmation of whether, and where, we are processing your personal data;
information about the categories of personal data we are processing, the purposes for which we process your personal data and information as to how we determine applicable retention periods;
information about the categories of recipients with whom we may share your personal data; and
a copy of the personal data we hold about you.
The right to receive a copy of the personal data you have provided to us in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format that supports re-use, or to request the transfer of your personal data to another person;
the right to know what data we process about you and any sales of your personal data and to whom;
the right to opt out of the future sales of your personal data to third parties;
the right to opt out of the future sharing of your personal data to third parties where your data is shared for commercial or similar benefit to a third party for the purposes of behavioural advertising;
the right to correct any inaccurate or incomplete personal data we hold about you;
the right, in some circumstances, to require us to delete your personal data;
the right, in some circumstances, to require us to limit the purposes for which we process your personal data if the continued processing of the personal data in this way is not justified, such as where the accuracy of the personal information is contested by you;
the right to appeal our decision if we decline to take action on a request exercising one of your rights set forth herein;
the right to non-discrimination for making a request.
